Abstract
A new family of metal-organic frameworks with qzd topology is proposed and exhaustively studied using multiscale computational analysis (grand canonical Monte Carlo; molecular mechanics; density functional theory) to reveal the structure-property relationships for predicting frameworks with high total methane uptake and working capacity. In our approach we take into account different linkers with triple bonds and/or benzene rings. Grand canonical Monte Carlo simulations demonstrate for several of the designed frameworks excellent methane storage properties, such as a balanced working capacity of $56\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $264\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at 5-80 bar and $240\mathrm{K}$ .
Keywords: MOF; sorption; methane
## Introduction
Transport around the world depends heavily on oil (the major part often being imported, for example in the case of Europe) and therefore has increasingly costly effects on the environment. Alternative fuels are needed to decrease the environmental impact. Natural gas (methane being the main component) is employed in automobile applications in liquefied (mostly trucks) and compressed (passenger cars) form, which is necessary due to very low energy density at ambient thermodynamic conditions when no permanent access to a pipeline is available. To be stored, compressed natural gas requires heavy cylinders and liquefied natural gas requires permanent cooling, both coming along with difficulties in the operation that limit wide usage. In automobiles the gas storage system has to operate at moderate thermodynamic conditions (240-320 K).1 Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are able to store much larger amounts of methane than an ordinary gas cylinder (at the same thermodynamic conditions) while achieving similar or even higher energy density.2-5 This means that automobiles can be refuelled without extra gas compression and/or the fuel tanks can be slimmed down.
High gravimetric and volumetric working capacities (difference between the total methane uptake at pressure 35 bar, 65 bar, or 80 bar and at pressure 5 bar5,6) of the storage material are key performance indicators. The US Department of Energy (DoE) therefore has formulated targets of $50\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $263~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ for efficient methane storage.7 As a tank will not contain one single crystal, powder of MOF crystallites must be compressed into specific shapes (for example, pellets) to minimize the void fraction that lowers the packing density as compared to a single crystal. Considering the energy density of compressed natural gas at 250 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , the DoE target takes into account a $25\%$ reduction for the volumetric working capacity. Recent results, both experimental and theoretical, indicate that it is almost impossible to achieve simultaneously the targets for both the gravimetric and volumetric methane working capacities by static adsorbent materials at room temperature.8 Al-soc-MOF-1 achieves at $258\mathrm{K}$ working capacities of $50\mathrm{wt\%}$ and 264 cm3 (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ . 9
Material simulations, including those in the form of computational screening, play a guiding role for the prediction of structure-property relationships,10 particularly in MOFs11-15. The strategy by Snurr et al. enables the design of MOFs from a library of chemical building blocks based on already known compounds and has been employed to enhance the methane working capacity16. Woo et al. have developed methods for quantitative evaluation of structure-property relationships using machine learning17. The structure-property relationship of ${\sim}130000$ hypothetical MOFs has been analyzed in Ref. 18 for methane storage at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 1, 35, and 100 bar. In Ref. 19 the maximal $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake has been addressed and a similar approach has been applied in Refs. 20 and 21 for obtaining the maximal methane uptake for different families of MOFs. Data mining of the Cambridge Structural Database has been employed by Siegel et al. to computationally screen ${\sim}20000$ MOFs for predicting $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage properties22. Several outstanding candidates have been identified and the authors have shown, for example, that the ratio of the gravimetric and volumetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ densities exhibits a maximum as a function of the surface area. Sastre et al. have reported large-scale computational screening of a database containing ${\sim}138000$ MOFs in order to identify candidates for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage23. 18 MOFs have been found that meet the DoE targets for both the gravimetric and volumetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ working capacities $(5.5\mathrm{wt\%,40\gL^{-1}})$ and, at the same time, have suitable pore volumes and pore size distributions7. In Ref. 24 a total of 122835 MOFs has been designed computationally to study the total methane uptake and working capacity as a function of the void fraction, volumetric surface area, and heat of adsorption, finding a maximal working capacity of $206\mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ . In the present work, we address a family of MOFs with qzd (Quartz dual) topology that has evaded attention so far. We analyse its methane sorption properties and show that the material performance surpasses that of any competitor MOF, achieving the challenging DoE targets.
## Structure simulation
The MOFs under consideration are built from Cu paddlewheel clusters and organic linkers. The parent B framework is obtained by combining molecular mechanics and density functional theory simulations, and afterwards employed to design seven qzd-MOFs by changing the linker, see Figure 1. To establish a topological pattern for the MOFs to be designed, we analyze the nets occurring in the previously reported paddlewheel based coordination polymers. To this purpose, we perform a complete screening of the Cambridge Structural Database (version 5.36, May 2015) and compile a list of 697 coordination polymers using the TOPOS package25. We then search in the Reticular Chemistry Structure Resource26 for uninodal nets not appearing in this list with the local vertex geometry closely resembling the plane square coordination of the metal atoms in paddlewheel cluster. It turns out that the qzd net (quarzt) does not occur in any of the previously reported polymers. We choose this net for building a new family of MOFs due to its simplicity and capability to form interpenetrated frameworks. Specifically, the series of qzd-MOFs is designed in the following steps:
1. Choosing organic linkers. For our isoreticular series we use ditopic linkers to limit the number of nets (uninodal 4- connected nets only) suitable for constructing candidate frameworks.
2. Finding high symmetry nets. Crystallographic data on the ideal qzd net is obtained from the Reticular Chemistry Structure Resource.
3. Building first archetypes. The construction consists of geometric calculations to place paddlewheel clusters onto net vertices and re-adjusting the unit cell parameters in accordance with the linear dimensions of cluster and linker. Linkers are placed on the edge centres. Clusters and linkers are aligned in the unit cell by minimizing the distances between the extension points of the clusters and those of the ligands.
4. Pre-optimizing the structures. Using molecular mechanics, we relax all atomic positions in each structure by the Forcite program of the Accelrys Materials Studio v6.1 package27. In these optimizations the unit cell parameters are kept fixed and the covalent bonds are described by means of the Universal Force Field (UFF)28. We use convergence thresholds (between optimization steps) of $10^{-4}$ kcal/mol, $5{\cdot}10^{-3}$ kcal/mol/Å, $5{\cdot}10^{-3}$ GPa, and $5{\cdot}10^{-5}\mathrm{\AA}$ for the energy, force, stress, and atomic displacements, respectively.
5. Designing the family. The parent frameworks from the previous step are utilized to build the family of qzd-MOFs by the molecular mechanics approach. These calculations again are based on the Forcite program of Materials Studio and the UFF parameters. Partial atomic charges are calculated by the QEq approach29. In the geometry optimizations the maximal accepted changes in the unit cell parameters are 0.01 Å.
6. Validating the unit cell parameters and atomic positions. Starting from the pre-optimized structure, we optimize the unit cell parameters by a series of constant energy cutoff calculations within density functional theory, changing the parameters by up to $5\%$ . For each set of parameters we perform a full relaxation of the atomic positions until the atomic forces have converged below $0.04~\mathrm{eV/\AA}$ . The obtained free energy values are fitted to the Birch-Murnaghan equation of state30 to find the equilibrium volume. We first optimize the cell volume and afterwards the $_{c/a}$ ratio for hexagonal cells and the $_{c/a}$ and $b/a$ ratios for orthorhombic cells. The first-principles calculations are carried out with the SIESTA program31 employing atomic orbital basis sets (double- $\cdot\zeta$ plus polarization). The valence charge density is calculated in the generalized gradient approximation (using the Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof functional) and the core electrons are described by norm-conserving pseudopotentials. All calculations consider only the $\Gamma$ -point. An energy cutoff of $600\mathrm{Ry}$ , defining the fineness of the real-space grid for calculating charge densities, is used. Convergence of the total energy with respect to this cutoff is verified. Agreement of the obtained structures (unit cell parameters and atomic positions) with those optimized by molecular mechanics validates the simulation model.
A schematic representation of the BB framework is shown in Figure 2 as example, where linkers of different layers are given in different colours for clarity. The Poreblazer program32 is used to derive the geometric surface area (N as probe), pore volume (He as probe), and framework density. The results in Table 1 show a strict correlation with the length of the linker. The stability of the qzd-MOFs is confirmed by deriving the elastic constants (by first-principles calculations33). According to the results given in Table 1, the stability criteria $\mathrm{C}_{44}>0$ , $\mathrm{C}_{11}>|\mathrm{C}_{12}|$ , $\mathrm{C}_{11}\mathrm{C}_{33}>(\mathrm{C}_{13})^{2}$ , and $\mathrm{C}_{33}(\mathrm{C}_{11}+\mathrm{C}_{12})>2(\mathrm{C}_{13})^{2}$ for crystals of hexagonal symmetry34 are fulfilled.
## Gas Sorption Simulation
We study the methane sorption properties by grand canonical Monte Carlo simulations at temperatures of 240 K, 258 K, 273 K, 298 K and pressures of up to 80 bar. Simulation parameters for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , presented as united atom, are taken from the TraPPE force field35 ( $\upsigma=3.73\textrm{\AA}$ , $\mathrm{\varepsilon/k_{B}}=148\mathrm{\K}$ ), as Ref. 9 has used this approach and achieved agreement with experiments for a MOF based on $\mathrm{cu}$ paddlewheels. The MOFs are described by the Dreiding force field36 and the sorption parameters for $\mathrm{Cu}$ are taken from the UFF. The Dreiding force field could not be used for the structure optimizations, as it does not include force constants for Cu. On the other hand, the UFF is known for overestimating the methane uptake, see Ref. 37, for example. Moreover, Lennard-Jones potentials are employed to describe the van der Waals interaction with a cut-off distance of $12.8\mathrm{\AA}$ . The framework is assumed to be rigid and periodic boundary conditions are applied. The MUSIC package $^{38}$ $2.5\times10^{7}$ steps for equilibration as well as production) and the PengRobinson equation of state39 are used. We note that Ref. 40 has adopted the same approach as implemented here to calculate the methane sorption isotherms of PCN-14 and found excellent agreement with experiments. We validate our methodology by comparing in Figure 3 experimental and simulated (by exactly the same methodology as used for the qzd-MOFs) methane sorption isotherms for state-of-the-art MOFs. It turns out that the simulations provide either excellent agreement with the experiment or conservative estimates, i.e., the MOFs discussed in the following are expected to perform even better than predicted here.
The T framework with the shortest linker exhibits the lowest gravimetric uptake at all temperatures studied ( $15\mathrm{\wt\%}$ at 80 bar and 298 K) but not the highest volumetric uptake (191 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at 80 bar and 298 K) even though it has the highest density among the frameworks under consideration, see Figure 4. The B framework exhibits a more balanced uptake of $24\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $221~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , both values being enhanced as compared to the T framework.
This enhancement can be explained as follows: The window dimensions of the ${\bf T}$ framework are $8.9\mathrm{~\AA~}\times7.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and $17.9\textup{\AA}\times7.8\textup{\AA}$ . Since the van der Waals radius of C is $1.7\mathrm{\AA}$ and the kinetic diameter of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is $3.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , only one $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecule fits in the windows $(7.8\mathrm{~\AA~{~-~}}2{\times}1.7\mathrm{~\AA~{~-~}}4.4\mathrm{~\AA~{~}~}$ . In the case of the $\mathbf{B}$ framework the window dimensions are $11.0\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times9.7\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ and $21.9\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\times9.7\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , which means that two $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules fit in the windows $(11.0\mathrm{\AA}-2{\times}1.7\mathrm{\AA}=7.6\$ Å). Comparison of the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ density in the two frameworks supports this argumentation. It is found to be $156\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{m}^{-3}$ $(\mathbf{T})$ and $159\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{m}^{-3}$ (B) at 35 bar, $187\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{m}^{-3}$ (T) and $203\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{m}^{-3}$ (B) at 65 bar, and $195\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{m}^{-3}$ (T) and $215\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{m}^{-3}$ (B) at 80 bar. Higher $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ density of the $\mathbf{B}$ framework, especially at higher pressure, reflects better packing of the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules. Thus, the volume is used more effectively and the gravimetric and volumetric uptakes are higher.
We turn our attention to the TT, TB, and BB frameworks, which exhibit moderate gravimetric uptakes of $29\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $34\mathrm{wt\%}$ , and $39\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively, at 80 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ . Interestingly, they provide similar volumetric uptakes of $197\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , $194~{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , and $185~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , respectively. Figure 4 demonstrates that at all temperatures and pressures the BB framework exhibits the highest gravimetric uptake, apart from the BTB and BBB frameworks, and almost the same volumetric uptake as the TT and TB frameworks (and additionally the B and T frameworks at $240\mathrm{K}$ ). We observe that the BTB and BBB frameworks provide the highest gravimetric uptakes of $48\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $53\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively, at 80 bar and $298\mathrm{K}$ . Unfortunately, their volumetric uptakes are the lowest under these thermodynamic conditions, $154~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and $147~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , respectively. At all temperatures, the simulations show that the length of the linker (high length $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ high pore volume and surface area but low framework density, see Table 1) always correlates with the gravimetric uptake, see Figure 4(a,c,e,g), while from the volumetric point of view only the TT, TB, BB, BTB, and BBB frameworks follow a common trend (the higher the density the higher is the uptake) but not the T and B frameworks, see Figure 4(b,d,f,h). Despite higher density of the T than the B framework the former provides lower volumetric uptakes at all temperatures because of its small surface area and pore volume, almost half of that of the B framework. Gas saturation thus occurs at lower pressure than in the case of the B framework (and all other frameworks). To show that the TraPPE force field is sufficient in the case of MOFs with charged $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions we validate our data by comparison to a nine-site model (as given in Table S5 of Ref. 41), see the sorption isotherm with open symbols in Figure 4(b). We find good agreement, indicating that the effects of the additional electrostatic terms of the nine-site model and additional secondary adsorption sites of the single-site model compensate each other.
