The BTB and BBB frameworks show the highest total methane gravimetric uptakes at 65 bar and $273\mathrm{~K~}$ , see Figure 6a. Balanced total methane uptakes are found for the TB ( $38~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $220~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ ) and BB ( $44\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $208~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ ) frameworks. Interestingly, we obtain impressive working capacities for the TT, TB, BB, BTB, and BBB frameworks at the same thermodynamic conditions, see Figure 6b. The best balanced performance is found for the TT $(32~\mathrm{wt^{\circ}/o},200~\mathrm{cm^{3}}~(\mathrm{STP})~\mathrm{cm^{-3}})$ and TB $35\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , $199\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ ) frameworks. Keeping the pressure at 65 bar, our results in Figure 6c,d for a reduced temperature of $240~\mathrm{K}$ demonstrate prominent working capacities for the BB $53\mathrm{\mt{\%}}$ , $252\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ ), BTB ( $73\mathrm{\mt{\%}}$ , $231\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ ), and BBB ( $79\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $219\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ ) frameworks.
Figure 7 addresses the gain in total methane uptake $(\mathrm{CH}_{4}@\ 80\ \mathrm{bar}-\mathrm{CH}_{4}\ @\ 65\ \mathrm{ban}$ ) and working capacity $\mathrm{\Delta}[\mathrm{CH}_{4}~\textcircled{a}$ 80 bar – CH4 @ 5 bar] – [CH4 @ 65 bar – CH4 @ 5 bar] = CH4 @ 80 bar – $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $@$ 65 bar) by pressure increase from 65 bar to 80 bar at $240~\mathrm{K}$ . Figure $\mathrm{7a}$ shows the trend that a longer linker corresponds to higher gain in total methane uptake. For the T framework with the shortest linker we obtain very small values of $0.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $8.9\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , which increase for the TT framework to $1.4\mathrm{wt^{0}}/\mathrm{o},9.7\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{(STP)cm^{-3}}$ . The TB framework results in gain of $2.1~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $11.7\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and the BB framework in gain of $2.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $12.6\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ . Because they have the longest linkers, the highest gains are obtained for the BTB and BBB frameworks: $7.6~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $24.0~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and 10.1 $w t\%$ , $28.1\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , respectively. Interestingly, the TT, TB, and BB frameworks show almost the same total methane volumetric uptakes at 65 bar (around $290\mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ and at 80 bar (around $300\mathrm{cm}^{3}\left(\mathrm{STP}\right)\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ , but very different total methane gravimetric uptakes (Figure 6a): $43.2~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 65 bar and $44.6~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 80 bar (TT), 50.9 $w t\%$ at 65 bar and $52.9~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 80 bar (TB), $60.0\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 65 bar and $62.6~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 80 bar (BB). The working capacity also follows the trend that longer linkers correspond to higher gain, see Figure 7b. The BTB and BBB frameworks thus exhibit the highest gains of $80.5\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , $255~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ and $89.2~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $247\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , respectively, for pressure increase from 65 bar to 80 bar. Potentially, these frameworks are suitable for applications that require high gravimetric working capacity. The ${\bf T}$ and $\mathbf{B}$ frameworks are omitted in Figure 7b, because their working capacities are low. The TT, TB, and BB frameworks show almost the same volumetric working capacities at 5-65 bar (around $250\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ (\mathrm{STP})\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ and at 5-80 bar (around $265\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ ), but very different gravimetric working capacities (Figure 6b): $37.6~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 65 bar and $39.1~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 80 bar (TT), $44.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 65 bar and $46.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 80 bar (TB), $53.0\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 65 bar and $55.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 80 bar (BB). All three achieve a significant gain in working capacity when the pressure increases (both gravimetric and volumetric). Remarkably, the BB framework exhibits at $240\mathrm{K}$ a balanced working capacity of $56\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $264~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ (STP) $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , suitable for industrial application (specifically automobile usage) in cold weather regions.
The isosteric heat of adsorption is calculated from the methane sorption isotherms at different temperatures using the integrated form of the Clausius-Clapeyron equation (see Refs. 50 and 51 for an alternative method),
$$
\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}=-\mathrm{~\bf{R}~}}&{{}\approx\mathrm{~\bf{R}~}\quad,}\end{array}
$$
where R is the real gas constant. The pressure p as a function of the temperature T at constant total methane uptake n is extracted from Figure 4. It turns out that $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ of the designed frameworks and benchmarking MOFs tends to decline for increasing surface area, see Table 2.
## Conclusions
We have designed MOFs with qzd topology by combining Cu paddlewheel clusters and organic linkers of different length. We have thoroughly analyzed the structure-property relationships in these qzd-MOFs, with a special focus on the methane storage properties at various thermodynamic conditions. Grand canonical Monte Carlo simulations have been used to show that qzd-MOFs can provide at low temperature outstanding total methane uptakes and working capacities. In particular, the BB framework satisfies the challenging DoE targets for the gravimetric and volumetric working capacities. The designed MOFs are proposed specifically for application in cold weather regions.
